<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barmakids</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Barmakid family, Barmakid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Barmakids were a family of Iranian origin, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> and administrators to the early Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. They held great influence under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>, patronising scholarship and organising government. In 803 Harun abruptly removed them from power, imprisoning and executing members of the family.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Barmakid family</b>, Persian administrators who served the early caliphs and rose to the height of their power as viziers under Harun al-Rashid until their sudden fall in 803, exemplified this multicultural elite.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Persian traditions of government and letters shaped the Abbasid court, whose most powerful family of ministers, the <b>Barmakids</b>, descended from the hereditary heads of a Buddhist monastery at Balkh.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Barmakids</b>, an Iranian family descended from the Buddhist chiefs of a monastery at Balkh, ran the state for much of Harun al-Rashid’s reign, until in 803 he imprisoned them and executed Ja’far.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
